<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.20.xsd">

  <!--
    PER-DOMAIN THEMES (operator ask 2026-08-14: "let themes be set different for
    different domains by the studio and by the superadmin or admin of a domain in the
    web ui").

    The mesh already themes ONE way for everyone: the Studio composes a token CSS and it
    is threaded to every UI as SNAP_THEME_CSS. That cannot express "this domain looks
    different", so the SELECTION has to become data the mesh owns rather than an env var
    baked at container start.

    ONE ROW PER DOMAIN, and the domain IS the primary key: a domain has exactly one
    theme, so a surrogate id would allow two rows for one domain and make "which wins?"
    a question the schema cannot answer. The EMPTY STRING (DomainTheme.MESH_DEFAULT) is a
    legal value and means the MESH DEFAULT — the theme a domain with no row of its own
    inherits.

    NOT PrincipalDomain.NO_DOMAIN, which this comment previously claimed was ''. It is
    the sentinel ' no-domain' (leading space), and that error was load-bearing: the
    default was written trimmed to 'no-domain' but read back untrimmed, so it resolved
    for nobody, and a principal whose tenant could NOT be resolved carried the same
    'no-domain' as its acting domain and so passed the ownership check on that very row.
    Everything keying this table now goes through DomainTheme.normalizeDomain.

    The selection is stored, NOT the rendered CSS: preset + ramp + style are what the
    operator chose, and re-deriving from them means a fix to the colour maths (the AA
    derivation has already been corrected twice) reaches every domain without a
    migration. A cached render belongs in front of this, never in it.
  -->
  <changeSet id="340-1-domain-theme" author="hugmesh">
    <createTable tableName="domain_theme">
      <column name="domain" type="varchar(255)">
        <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
      </column>
      <!-- theme_presets.PRESETS ids ("nord", "pride", "dark"). Not an enum: the preset
           table is data and grows (12 desktop schemes landed in one commit), and an
           enum would need a migration per addition. -->
      <column name="preset" type="varchar(64)">
        <constraints nullable="false"/>
      </column>
      <!-- theme_presets.MODES — the time-of-day ramp (dark|light|dawn|…). -->
      <column name="ramp" type="varchar(32)" defaultValue="dark">
        <constraints nullable="false"/>
      </column>
      <!-- theme.STYLE_VARIANTS — the shape axis (soft|sharp|outlined|…). -->
      <column name="style" type="varchar(32)" defaultValue="soft">
        <constraints nullable="false"/>
      </column>
      <!-- Optional hand-picked overrides; null = use the preset's own colours. -->
      <column name="primary_hex" type="varchar(7)"/>
      <column name="secondary_hex" type="varchar(7)"/>
      <column name="updated_at" type="timestamp" defaultValueComputed="now()">
        <constraints nullable="false"/>
      </column>
      <!-- WHO last changed it. A theme is a visible, mesh-wide change and the audit
           question ("who made everything pink?") has no other answer. -->
      <column name="updated_by" type="varchar(255)"/>
    </createTable>
  </changeSet>

  <!-- Shape guards. A bad value here does not fail loudly at write time — it renders a
       fallback theme, which reads as "my choice was ignored", the exact complaint this
       whole feature line has already produced twice. Reject it at the boundary. -->
  <changeSet id="340-2-domain-theme-hex-check" author="hugmesh">
    <sql>
      ALTER TABLE domain_theme ADD CONSTRAINT domain_theme_primary_hex_chk
        CHECK (primary_hex IS NULL OR primary_hex ~ '^#[0-9A-Fa-f]{6}$');
      ALTER TABLE domain_theme ADD CONSTRAINT domain_theme_secondary_hex_chk
        CHECK (secondary_hex IS NULL OR secondary_hex ~ '^#[0-9A-Fa-f]{6}$');
    </sql>
    <rollback>
      <sql>
        ALTER TABLE domain_theme DROP CONSTRAINT domain_theme_primary_hex_chk;
        ALTER TABLE domain_theme DROP CONSTRAINT domain_theme_secondary_hex_chk;
      </sql>
    </rollback>
  </changeSet>

  <!-- The runtime role. Without this every query on domain_theme fails in PRODUCTION
       with "permission denied for table domain_theme", while every test passes — the
       Testcontainers harness connects as the container superuser, not as admin_bff, so
       a missing GRANT is invisible to it. That is the same blind spot the H2 store had
       (it ignored GRANTs outright), and the reason the other tables' GRANTs exist.

       DELETE is granted, following fleet_entitlement rather than the audit tables: a
       theme override is revocable state — clearing one is how a domain goes back to
       inheriting the mesh default — not an audit trail. -->
  <changeSet id="340-3-domain-theme-grant" author="hugmesh">
    <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN" onError="MARK_RAN">
      <dbms type="postgresql"/>
    </preConditions>
    <comment>GRANT SELECT,INSERT,UPDATE,DELETE on domain_theme to the admin_bff role. PG
             only; MARK_RAN elsewhere.</comment>
    <sql>GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON domain_theme TO admin_bff</sql>
    <rollback>REVOKE SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON domain_theme FROM admin_bff</rollback>
  </changeSet>

  <!--
    THE RENDERED CSS, AND WHY IT IS A CACHE RATHER THAN THE TRUTH.

    The selection above stays authoritative; this column is derived from it. That
    distinction is the whole reason the selection is stored at all — a fix to the colour
    maths (already corrected twice: the sd/hug token-prefix bug, and accent text falling
    below AA on the lighter ramps) must reach every domain by re-rendering, not by a data
    migration. Dropping this column loses nothing but a re-render.

    So why store it at all? Because the maths has exactly ONE implementation, in Python
    (studio/hugmesh_studio/core/theme.py + theme_presets.py), and it must stay that way.
    The entrypoint hook that ships today already states the rule: it interpolates
    already-derived token text and does zero colour maths, because "a shell re-derivation
    would be a second implementation whose drift makes the Studio preview lie". Deriving
    in Java here would be exactly that second implementation, with the same drift and a
    worse failure — the admin UI would show one theme and the page would render another.

    So the renderer PUSHES its output and this BFF only serves bytes it was handed. A
    NULL means "not rendered yet", which serves empty CSS and therefore the shipped
    default chrome — byte-identical to today's behaviour when SNAP_THEME_CSS is unset.
  -->
  <changeSet id="340-4-domain-theme-rendered-css" author="hugmesh">
    <addColumn tableName="domain_theme">
      <!-- text, not varchar: a full token sheet is ~7 KB today and the palette/style
           axes only add to it. A length cap here would truncate a theme into an
           invalid stylesheet, which fails as a silently half-applied brand. -->
      <column name="rendered_css" type="text"/>
      <!-- WHICH renderer produced it, so a stale cache is identifiable rather than
           merely suspected when the maths changes. -->
      <column name="rendered_by" type="varchar(64)"/>
    </addColumn>
  </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
